        <section id="wrapping-it-up" style="background: var(--bg-card, #ffffff); border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 16px; padding: 24px; margin-bottom: 32px; text-align: center; width: 100%; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.03);">
          <h2 style="font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 0 0 10px 0; color: #2A680D;">Wrapping Up</h2>
          <p style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #09090b; margin-bottom: 16px; max-width: 850px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; text-align: left;">
            Splunk did not suddenly get worse in 2026. The math of ingestion-based pricing simply forced more teams to confront a cost curve that was always going to catch up with them eventually, and the Cisco acquisition gave a lot of renewal conversations a reason to happen a little earlier than they otherwise would have. Whether you prefer open-source control through Wazuh or the Elastic Stack, cloud native consolidation through Microsoft Sentinel or Google Security Operations, or a managed enterprise replacement through Sumo Logic, QRadar, or Exabeam, the alternative market is mature enough in 2026 that leaving Splunk no longer means compromising meaningfully on capability.
          </p>
          <p style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #09090b; margin-bottom: 16px; max-width: 850px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; text-align: left;">
